  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 231 PM CDT Fri Jul 24 2026\n\nPotential severe weather remains the primary forecast challenge for \nthis afternoon. WAA off an amplifying ridge over the Rockies, along \nwith a passing weak surface frontal boundary, may help to \ninitiate thunderstorms this afternoon. With rapidly rising \ntemperatures in the southwest and south central, capping would \nseem like less of an issue as indicated by the SPC mesoanalysis \npage. This would also explain why some CAMs are initiating \nconvection further west than previously thought. That said, the \n18Z KBIS sounding shows very dry air in place, while not even a \nfew bubbles of a cu field are present on satellite. What also \nseems interesting is that the left exit region of the upper \nlevel jet appears to be located closer to the International \nBorder. Overall, there remains a conditional severe threat for \nthe forecast area, though wondering if the better dynamics\noverall will wind up lining up further north and east.\nNevertheless, with ample MLCAPE and plenty of deep layer shear,\nthere remains plenty of opportunity for severe weather this \nafternoon and early evening.\n\nThe primary threats for today remain wind gusts of 70 mph and \ngolf ball size hail. In regard to the tornado threat, while\nmodel forecast hodographs suggest some curvature in the lower \nlevels mainly over the south central east of the Missouri River,\nLCLs are also increasing. This overall seems to favor strong \nwind gusts over tornadogenesis from the James River Valley and\nwest. Still, cannot completely rule out a tornado if\nthunderstorms were to persist into the evening hours in the\nJames River Valley.\n\nOnce any thunderstorms clear out this evening, dry conditions \nare expected tonight through Saturday. A few isolated \nthunderstorms are possible Saturday night and Sunday, though \nsevere weather is not anticipated at this time. With the \namplifying ridge sliding further east, expect very hot \nconditions to return Saturday and Sunday. Very dry conditions \nalong and west of the Missouri River will see minimum relative \nhumidity drop down into the teens (possibly low 20s in the \nMissouri River Valley) off mid 40 to mid 50 degree dewpoints, \nwhile highs reach up to 105. East of the Missouri River Valley \nwill see highs up to 100 albeit with dewpoints in the 60s. With \nthese very hot conditions, issued a Heat Advisory for Saturday \nafternoon through the day Sunday. In the far southwest where the\ndriest conditions are anticipated, near critical fire weather \nconditions are expected this afternoon and possibly again on \nSunday. \n\nFor next week, above average temperatures are expected to\ncontinue. While there might be a slight \"cooling\" trend, NBM\nforecasts are still mostly in the 90s. Periodic low shower and\nthunderstorm chances are in the forecast for next week, with the\nhighest probabilities during the middle of the week. A few\nstorms could be severe on any given day, though no day next week \nis currently worthy of enhanced messaging at this time.",
